The only argument for keeping everything inline in the constructor is avoiding function call overhead, and that's absurd here — construction happens once for the lifetime of the process. There is zero performance justification for inlining this work. What there is, is a readability cost that you correctly identified: the constructor is hard to follow because you have to mentally parse what each block of code is doing rather than reading a name that tells you.

Your instinct to push the logic into named private methods is the right call. The constructor becomes a sequence of clearly named steps, each method is independently readable and testable, and if you ever add a second constructor, the shared logic lives in reusable methods rather than being duplicated or extracted after the fact.

On your point about `PoolConfig` doing more work — I think there's a valid split here. Pure validation of the raw inputs (are any zeros, is alignment a power of two) could reasonably live on `PoolConfig` itself as a `validate()` method, since those checks are about whether the configuration is well-formed regardless of who consumes it. The derived computations (rounding block size, rounding chunk size, computing blocks per chunk) are pool-specific transformations that belong on `ProcessMemoryPool` as private methods, since those computations exist to serve the pool's internal layout.

