chat_messages.text:
 **On the end-past-last convention**

Yes, this is the standard convention in C and C++. The pattern is called a half-open range — the start is inclusive, the end is exclusive. Written as `[base, end)` in mathematical notation.

The reason this is universal in C++ rather than "end means last valid address" comes down to how it simplifies every operation that works with ranges.

Loop termination becomes a simple inequality. `cursor < end` means "still in bounds." If `end` pointed to the last valid byte, you'd need `cursor <= end`, and then you'd have to think carefully about whether advancing `cursor` past `end` before the next comparison is safe. With one-past-the-end, the pointer `end` itself is never dereferenced — it's only used for comparison. The standard explicitly says you may compute and compare a pointer one past the end of an array without dereferencing it.

Size calculation is clean subtraction. `end - base` gives you the exact number of bytes in the region. If `end` pointed to the last valid byte, you'd need `end - base + 1`, and off-by-one errors would creep in everywhere.

Empty ranges work naturally. When `base == end`, the range is empty — there are zero elements. If `end` meant "last valid element," an empty range would be impossible to represent without a separate flag or sentinel.

This is the same convention the standard library uses everywhere. `std::vector::end()`, `std::span::end()`, `std::string::end()`, iterator ranges in algorithms — all one-past-the-end. So when you write `data()` and `end()` on your chunk descriptor, anyone familiar with C++ will immediately understand the contract.

**The revised surface**

