A few notes on what I did.

`data()` and `end()` use `static_cast` rather than `reinterpret_cast` because casting from `void*` to any other pointer type is a well-defined `static_cast` in C++. `reinterpret_cast` would also work but `static_cast` is the narrower and more appropriate tool here.

`containsAddress()` casts the incoming pointer to `const std::byte*` so the comparisons are between the same type. The null check is first and returns early — a null pointer is never contained by any region regardless of what the region describes.

`isAlignedTo()` is the one place where `reinterpret_cast` is necessary. You can't do modular arithmetic on a pointer directly — you have to convert it to an integer type first. `std::uintptr_t` is the standard integer type guaranteed to hold a pointer value. The modulo check works because alignment is always a power of two, so if the remainder is zero the address is aligned.

`end()` computes the result from `base_` each time rather than caching it. The addition is a single instruction and the method is `noexcept` and trivially inlineable — the compiler will almost certainly inline it and the cost is unmeasurable. Caching would mean a fourth member that's derivable from two others, which is redundant state to maintain.

Everything returns by value, nothing allocates, nothing can fail. These are all leaf operations on scalar data.

