 You're right. The relaxed ordering on failure isn't "no side effects" — the CAS *does* update the expected value parameter with the current head, which is a meaningful side effect that the loop depends on. What I should have said is that the failure path doesn't need to synchronize memory visibility with other threads because we're just going to retry immediately with the corrected value. The relaxed ordering is sufficient because we don't act on any shared data based on the failed comparison — we just loop.

Here's the revised version with the heading comment:

```cpp
// Prepend the new node onto the tracking list via CAS. On failure,
// the expected value (node->next) is automatically updated to the
// current head, correcting the link for the next attempt.
while (
    !allocListHead_.compare_exchange_weak(
        newAllocNode_ptr->next,      ///< Expected current head; updated on failure.
        newAllocNode_ptr,            ///< New head to install on success.
        std::memory_order_release,   ///< Success: publish node writes to other threads.
        std::memory_order_relaxed    ///< Failure: retry with corrected next pointer.
    )
) { /* CAS retry */ }
```

The failure comment now describes what actually happens — the next pointer gets corrected and we retry — rather than claiming nothing happened.

