 There's a problem with the pointer arithmetic. `FreeRegionNode*` arithmetic advances in units of `sizeof(FreeRegionNode)`, not bytes. When you write `currentBlock + blockByteSize`, you're advancing by `blockByteSize * sizeof(FreeRegionNode)` bytes, not by `blockByteSize` bytes.

Same issue with `chainHead + chunk.size().size_t_bytes()` for the end pointer — that advances by `chunk_size * sizeof(FreeRegionNode)` bytes, which is way past the end of the chunk.

This is exactly why my version used `std::byte*` for the cursor and end pointer — `std::byte` is one byte, so pointer arithmetic on `std::byte*` advances in single bytes, which is what you want when stepping by an arbitrary byte count.

The fix is to do the arithmetic in `std::byte*` and cast to `FreeRegionNode*` only when you need to write the next pointer:

```cpp
void ProcessMemoryPool::carve_and_link(const MemoryRegion& chunk)
{
    const size_t blockByteSize = config_.block_size.size_t_bytes();

    std::byte* cursor = static_cast<std::byte*>(chunk.base());
    std::byte* end = cursor + chunk.size().size_t_bytes();

    auto* chainHead = reinterpret_cast<FreeRegionNode*>(cursor);
    FreeRegionNode* chainTail = nullptr;

    while (cursor < end) {
        auto* currentBlock = reinterpret_cast<FreeRegionNode*>(cursor);
        std::byte* nextBlockAddr = cursor + blockByteSize;

        if (nextBlockAddr < end) {
            currentBlock->next = reinterpret_cast<FreeRegionNode*>(nextBlockAddr);
        }
        else {
            currentBlock->next = nullptr;
            chainTail = currentBlock;
        }

        cursor = nextBlockAddr;
    }

    push_batch(chainHead, chainTail);
}
```

