There it is. Yes, let's convert `push_batch` to match. Here's the GCC path for its CAS:

```cpp
#if CHEVRON_MSVC
        swapSuccess = _InterlockedCompareExchange128(
            reinterpret_cast<volatile int64_t*>(&free_list_head_),
            static_cast<int64_t>(newHead.swaps),
            reinterpret_cast<int64_t>(newHead.ptr),
            reinterpret_cast<int64_t*>(&currentHead)
        ) == 1;
#elif CHEVRON_GCC
        {
            __int128 expected_val = *reinterpret_cast<__int128*>(&currentHead);
            __int128 desired_val = *reinterpret_cast<__int128*>(&newHead);
            __int128 old_val = __sync_val_compare_and_swap(
                reinterpret_cast<__int128*>(&free_list_head_),
                expected_val,
                desired_val
            );
            swapSuccess = (old_val == expected_val);
            if (!swapSuccess) {
                memcpy(&currentHead, &old_val, sizeof(currentHead));
            }
        }
#else
        swapSuccess = __atomic_compare_exchange(
            &free_list_head_,
            &currentHead,
            &newHead,
            true,
            __ATOMIC_RELEASE,
            __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE
        );
#endif
```

Identical pattern. Build and check — you should see two `lock cmpxchg16b` instructions now.

The question is whether you want to also force inline `cmpxchg16b` for the load, or accept the `libatomic` dispatch for that one call.

On x86-64, there is no 128-bit atomic load instruction. The only way to atomically read 128 bits is `lock cmpxchg16b` — even `libatomic`'s `__atomic_load_16` uses it internally. So the operation is the same either way, the only difference is whether it's inlined or dispatched.

The load happens once per method call, before the CAS loop. It's not the hot path — the loop is. So the `libatomic` dispatch overhead on the load is a one-time function call cost, not a per-iteration cost.

That said, for consistency you might want everything going through the same inline path. If so, the GCC load becomes the same trick as MSVC — a CAS-as-load:

```cpp
#if CHEVRON_MSVC
    _InterlockedCompareExchange128(
        reinterpret_cast<volatile int64_t*>(&free_list_head_),
        0, 0,
        reinterpret_cast<int64_t*>(&currentHead)
    );
#elif CHEVRON_GCC
    {
        __int128 zero = 0;
        __int128 old_val = __sync_val_compare_and_swap(
            reinterpret_cast<__int128*>(&free_list_head_),
            zero,
            zero
        );
        memcpy(&currentHead, &old_val, sizeof(currentHead));
    }
#else
    __atomic_load(&free_list_head_, &currentHead, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
#endif
```

The logic: compare against zero, exchange with zero. If the head is zero, the CAS succeeds and writes zero — no change. If the head is non-zero, the CAS fails and returns the actual value. Either way, `old_val` holds the current head.

Your call — consistency with the rest of the GCC path, or leave the load going through `libatomic` since it's not in the loop.

