What this enables: simplicity. The scope stays focused on block management. Strategies are flexible — stack-allocated, member variables, dynamically allocated, whatever fits the context. No registry overhead on the scope. A strategy is just a type that happens to draw blocks from a scope.

What this prevents: the scope can't iterate its strategies for diagnostics ("how much memory is each strategy using?"). It can't enforce teardown ordering — if a strategy outlives its scope, it holds dangling block references. It can't do bulk operations ("flush all strategies", "report total usage across strategies"). It can't mediate between strategies ("this arena is idle, reclaim its blocks for the pool"). And critically for your ecosystem vision — there's no integration point. Strategies are isolated from each other and from the scope's awareness. You can't build an ecosystem on disconnected parts.

**Managed Model — thorough assessment**

Strategies are attached to the scope. The scope knows what strategies exist on it, can manage their lifecycle, and provides an integration surface.

What this enables: the scope becomes the coordination point for its strategies. It can report aggregate diagnostics. It can enforce teardown ordering — strategies are destroyed before blocks are released. It can mediate — if a strategy returns blocks to the scope, those blocks are available to other strategies on the same scope. It can provide discovery — given a scope, you can query what strategies are available and access them by type. This is the foundation for an ecosystem where strategies collaborate through their shared scope.

What it costs: the scope is more complex. It needs a way to store heterogeneous strategy types, which raises the type erasure question.

**The type storage question — and a Chevron parallel**

Storing multiple strategies of different types on a scope is the same problem that `WindowEnvelope` solved with `WindowComponentStore`. Each `WindowEnvelope` holds typed components keyed by `std::type_index`, each type stored once, retrieved by type. The pattern is established in Chevron and proven.

A `MemoryScope` could use the same approach. Each strategy type is attached once. Retrieval is by type:

```cpp
scope.attach<BumpArena>(arena_config);
scope.attach<FixedPool>(pool_config);

auto& arena = scope.get<BumpArena>();
auto& pool = scope.get<FixedPool>();
```

No virtual dispatch for allocation — you get back the concrete type and call its methods directly. The type-keyed store handles lifetime and discovery. The scope destroys strategies in reverse attachment order during teardown.

**Where CRTP fits**

CRTP gives every strategy a common compile-time base without virtual overhead:

```cpp
template <typename Derived>
class MemoryStrategy {
protected:
    MemoryScope& scope_;

    MemoryRegion acquire_backing_block() {
        return scope_.acquire_block();
    }

    void release_backing_block(MemoryRegion& block) {
        scope_.release_block(block);
    }

public:
    MemoryScope& scope() const noexcept { return scope_; }
    // Common diagnostics every strategy shares
    units::Bytes memory_held() const noexcept;
    std::size_t blocks_held() const noexcept;
};
```

Every strategy inherits from `MemoryStrategy<Self>`. The base provides the unified scope interaction — acquiring and releasing blocks — plus common diagnostics. The derived type provides the specific allocation interface. No virtual dispatch anywhere. The scope's type-keyed store holds the derived types directly.

This gives you the unified surface you're looking for. No matter what strategy, it holds memory from a scope, can report how much it holds, and acquires/releases blocks through the same base interface. The specific allocation mechanics (bump, pool, free-list) are on the derived type where they belong.

**Both models coexisting**

These aren't mutually exclusive. The CRTP base holds a scope reference regardless of how the strategy was created. The managed path gives you integration:

```cpp
// Managed — attached to scope, scope-aware, discoverable
scope.attach<BumpArena>(config);
auto& arena = scope.get<BumpArena>();
```

The independent path gives you flexibility for cases where attachment doesn't make sense:

```cpp
// Independent — standalone, not scope-tracked
BumpArena temp_arena(scope, config);
// Used locally, destroyed locally
```

The CRTP base is the same in both cases. The difference is whether the scope tracks the strategy or not. Managed strategies get lifecycle management and discovery. Independent strategies get simplicity and locality.

**My assessment**

For Chevron's ecosystem vision, the managed model with CRTP is the right backbone. It gives you the integration surface to build on — diagnostics, lifecycle, discovery, mediation. The independent model survives as an option for lightweight, temporary, or local-scope strategies where attachment overhead isn't warranted.

The CRTP base keeps the allocation hot path clean — no virtual dispatch, just a direct method call on the concrete strategy type. The scope's type-keyed store (parallel to `WindowComponentStore`) handles the heterogeneous storage without type erasure on the allocation path.

Where do you want to go from here — the CRTP base design, the scope's strategy store, or the scope's public API that supports both models?

The cleanest mechanism for this in C++ is a compile-time threading policy as a template parameter on the strategy. The user declares their intent at the type level — no assumptions, no runtime checks, no paying for what you don't use.

Two policy types:

```cpp
struct SingleThreaded {
    using FreeList = PlainFreeList;
};

struct MultiThreaded {
    using FreeList = AtomicFreeList;
};
```

The CRTP base is parameterized on the policy:

```cpp
template <typename Derived, typename Threading = SingleThreaded>
class MemoryStrategy { ... };
```

The user's choice is explicit and visible:

```cpp
scope.attach<BumpArena<SingleThreaded>>(config);   // no atomics internally
scope.attach<BumpArena<MultiThreaded>>(config);     // CAS-based internally
```

No assumptions about what strategies are used where. No runtime branching. The compiler generates exactly the right code for each variant. The policy determines the free list type and any internal counter types the strategy uses. The scope interaction (popping from the scope's `AtomicFreeList`) is always atomic regardless of policy, because the scope is shared. The policy only governs the strategy's own internal block management.

This is standard policy-based design — well-established pattern, zero overhead, explicit user intent. The threading choice is part of the type, so you can't accidentally share a single-threaded strategy across threads without it being visible in the type system.

